{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And I will command My blessing, etc.</b> This blessing is not upon the growth, for He has already promised ״וְנָתְנָה הָאָרֶץ פִּרְיָהּ״ — which is the limit that HaShem set for its natural power. Rather, He promises a blessing of the produce like the jar of meal of the Zarephath widow (I Kings 17:16) and the oil of the wife of Obadiah (II Kings 4:7). This is what He was precise in saying <b>and it shall accomplish the produce</b> — meaning, the blessing that HaShem commands shall cause a multiplication in the produce so that it suffices for three years, even though the earth puts forth its growth as it does every year. Go and learn from the kav of wheat in the upper story (Ta'anit 24a), and see what I explained on the verse ״וְכִי יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ (Genesis 33:11)." "<b>for three years.</b> The plain sense of the verse is for the year itself, which is the sixth, and the seventh year, and the eighth year. On this reckoning: from the month of Sivan of the sixth year to the month of Sivan of the seventh and of the eighth is two years, and from the month of Sivan of the eighth to Sivan of the ninth is three years. And this is the verse's statement <b>until the ninth year</b> (Leviticus 25:22) — not until the beginning of the ninth but until its produce arrives; behold, three years from day to day. And the verse needed to explain, lest you think that a partial year is counted as a year."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:15.291077602Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}